    {
      "id": "r20261102-a02",
      "title": "The Rise of Silent Book Clubs",
      "level": "Menengah atas",
      "min": 4,
      "body": [
        "Book clubs usually mean long discussions about one book everyone had to read. But a new kind of book club is becoming popular, and in these clubs, nobody talks about the book at all.",
        "In a silent book club, people meet in a cafe, a library, or a park. For the first thirty minutes, they drink tea and chat about anything. Then, for one hour, everyone reads their own book in silence. There is no homework, no pressure to finish, and no arguing about which book is best. Introverts especially love this format because they can enjoy being around people without having to talk.",
        "Silent book clubs started in Seattle and have spread to hundreds of cities. For many members, it is the perfect mix: time alone, together."
      ],
      "gloss": {
        "discussions": "diskusi",
        "silent": "hening",
        "pressure": "tekanan",
        "arguing": "berdebat",
        "introverts": "introvert",
        "format": "format",
        "spread": "menyebar"
      },
      "qs": [
        {
          "q": "What happens during the middle hour of a silent book club meeting?",
          "opts": [
            "Everyone reads their own book in silence",
